Subject: Problems resolving Windows Icon position in more recent releases 
of Windows (part 2)
To: pepswin <Unknown>


In part one, we discussed part of why there are so many icons in the PEPS 
main COMMAND window, including the need for at least five different 
operating system launchers, the need to launchan auxiliary SETUP commands 
window, and the beginning of an explanation for why there are many pairs of 
icons concerning nearly every individual user project file.

When you use an Edit *<objectfilename> *command, one way is to perform the command 
is to edit the file and then exit.  A copy of the file is then copied to 
the input stream file to SIMH for reading paper-tape.

When SIMH is run from whatever choice of operating system, it is expected 
that the system user chose the appropriate utility.

In OS/8, that would generally be PIP to read from PTR: into a text file 
such as FOOBAR.PA.

Note: The OS/8 PTR: handler has a serious bug.  The very last character is 
never transferred.  This can be dealt with in several ways:

a) Perhaps ignore the trailing <LF> character after the last <CR> which 
prematurely ends the file.  If the only point in running this OS/8 session 
in SIMH is to assemble the program [which is recommended; the example uses 
host editing in Windows] then fortunately there is not  a problem.  OS/8 
ends lines on <CR> and ignores <LF>.  Other OS/8 programs may do the 
opposite and might complain.

b) Expecting this behavior, use OS/8 TECO which will properly end the file 
even if both the <CR> as well as the <LF> are missing.  Any additional work 
is now being done on the intended file.

The bug is unfixable without a serious redesign of the PTR: handler that 
would then break the handler on real-world PTR: hardware.

c) If P?S/8 is booted to using the OS/8 BOOT/DT command, there are means to 
convert the file between the two systems in both directions of conversion.

The P?S/8 conversion program OS8CON ignores <CR> and uses <LF> unless the 
<CR> is missing [or both at the end-of-file.  After the file is converted, 
it can be converted back in place to where it is on an OS/8 DECtape and the 
problem is solved.  A trivial P?S/8 BATCH command file exists to perform 
this very function; I use it all the time.

The P?S/8 PTUTIL paper-tape text conversion program [there are separate 
utilities for RIM and BIN format binary tapes] supports an option to allow 
operation both on the real hardware and the SIMH simulated hardware which 
behaves slightly differently.

The reason for the problem is that when OS/8 was in its heyday. paper-tape 
was already backward and hardly used; it always expected that all actual 
paper-tapes have leader and trailer frames.  While OS/8's PTP: output can 
add paper-tape leader and trailer frames, this simply does not exist in the 
SIMH/PEPS environment and was simply overlooked.  Note: If this were 
changes, some binary paper tapes would become corrupted!  Paper-tape as a 
means of "injecting" files into the simulated environment was co-opted not 
really concerned with this then unknown issue.  P?S/8 is more flexible and 
can embrace all options required, etc.

The second and preferable way to use the edit process is to SAVE the 
project file but leave the editor open with the file intact.  In that case 
use the associated SEND *<objectfilename>* command which copies the 
previously saved file to the PTR: stream file and then also runs the chosen 
operating system variant from all five of the choices.  Every underlying 
batch file has all choices roughly analogous to how the EDIT function's 
underlying batch command file has ultimate overriding choice of editing 
program over all other settings, etc.

If the file is processed by a language utility such as PAL or FOCAL, the 
results can be sent back to Windows for examination of for example a PAL 
listing file, and then appropriate additional edits are made in the still 
opened editor program

This by far the most effective way to use a simulated environment.  
However, the user chooses what is most comfortable, etc.

Thus, this is why the Commands window will tend to have pairs of icons for 
each project and it quickly can easily fill up and require user-preferenced 
icon positioning.

Note: There is an alternate way to approach editing in a more generic way, 
however, the object of the edit must be a one-off choice; it is easily 
changed but adds FIVE more icons, two as per each other file project, one 
to set the default file to work with, one to list the generic file 
directory,and another to setup to navigate that directory.  There is also 
other icons concerned with package overall issues and access to the SIMH 
log file, which can be very useful.  One of the setup options is to 
enable/disable the log file and whether or not to clear it,etc.  There are 
two more icons for a choice of PDF conversion of the LPT output with or 
without greenbar, and two sets of three icons to maintain the output of the 
LPT and three more for the PTP high-speed punch output.

I'm sure I've forgotten to mention  a few more icons, but the main point is 
you need a LOT of icons, a means to custom position them that is personal 
preference and differs from the clumsy Windows default of both auto-arrange 
and align/snap to an imaginary grid you cannot disable.

This capability also exists in Windows Vista, but was removed from Windows 
7 and following!

In part 3 we will discuss the release-dependent problems of Windows 7, 8 
and 10, which unfortunately aren't either cooperative or even the same!



